(7413634, 1168535, 32108, '<td valign="top">"Where is she", Angel thought, she only had 30 minutes left on her lunch break and they would never make it. At the next table Alberto pondered how he was going to break the news to his wife.</td>', '"Where is she", Angel thought, she only had 30 minutes left on her lunch break and they would never make it. At the next table Alberto pondered how he was going to break the news to his wife.', '2015-11-02 06:50:10.000000', NULL, 1),
(7413641, 1168762, 43463, '<td valign="top">A thoughtful capture that presents itself as a study of social interactions. I find it interesting to note that in today''s world of technology one is more likely to find people of any age taking a selfie <b><u>facing away </u></b>from the art on the wall rather than face towards the painting/artwork to contemplate its beauty/meaning. I also find it a little ironic how they are intent on ''documenting'' their life &amp; experiences while you are essentially doing the same thing in this street capture. The sepia toning gives this capture a feeling of age - recalling a pleasant time in years past.</td>', 'A thoughtful capture that presents itself as a study of social interactions. I find it interesting to note that in today''s world of technology one is more likely to find people of any age taking a selfie facing away from the art on the wall rather than face towards the painting/artwork to contemplate its beauty/meaning. I also find it a little ironic how they are intent on ''documenting'' their life & experiences while you are essentially doing the same thing in this street capture. The sepia toning gives this capture a feeling of age - recalling a pleasant time in years past.', '2015-11-02 08:11:31.000000', NULL, 1),
(7413642, 1168704, 43463, '<td valign="top">On second viewing I noticed the sticker on the wheelchair - what a serendipitous find with this street scene! The ''elephant in the room'' is the way society looks at or treats those who are handicapped - for many it is an uncomfortable truth that is either ignored or goes unaddressed. Here in this photograph the man in the wheelchair sits alone - no one is talking to him, smiling with him, or sitting beside him. In fact the camera shows us a person who is walking away. There are people far in the distance and that distance shows the ''disconnect'' that is going on here between the man in the wheelchair and the society he lives in.</td>', 'On second viewing I noticed the sticker on the wheelchair - what a serendipitous find with this street scene! The ''elephant in the room'' is the way society looks at or treats those who are handicapped - for many it is an uncomfortable truth that is either ignored or goes unaddressed. Here in this photograph the man in the wheelchair sits alone - no one is talking to him, smiling with him, or sitting beside him. In fact the camera shows us a person who is walking away. There are people far in the distance and that distance shows the ''disconnect'' that is going on here between the man in the wheelchair and the society he lives in.', '2015-11-02 08:20:33.000000', NULL, 1),
(7413643, 1168793, 43463, '<td valign="top">I like the juxaposition of main subjects here. The road can take on the symbolical meaning of a road traveled: be it home, leaving home, travel in or to a simpler time, etc. There are three travelers on the road. The car travels away off to the horizon while the two horse riders are traveling towards the viewer. To me, I feel the main focus/message is that these two horse riders are ''returning'' home; back to less hectic lifestyle. They are turning away from technology (the car) and figuratively and literally ''enjoying the ride''. By going a slower pace (heh, less horsepower than a fast car engine) one notices/enjoys the surroundings of nature around them.</td>', 'I like the juxaposition of main subjects here. The road can take on the symbolical meaning of a road traveled: be it home, leaving home, travel in or to a simpler time, etc. There are three travelers on the road. The car travels away off to the horizon while the two horse riders are traveling towards the viewer. To me, I feel the main focus/message is that these two horse riders are ''returning'' home; back to less hectic lifestyle. They are turning away from technology (the car) and figuratively and literally ''enjoying the ride''. By going a slower pace (heh, less horsepower than a fast car engine) one notices/enjoys the surroundings of nature around them.', '2015-11-02 08:29:16.000000', NULL, 1),
(7413645, 1168799, 43463, '<td valign="top">Great capture of this gentleman taking a seat and contemplating his surroundings. The lighting and B&amp;W tones are wonderful. I think that this contemplative portrait of this man would be even more stronger if the main subject was fully in the frame from head to toe in an vertical frame. The horizontal crop has a little too much negative space. A vertical composition could still have some the street surroundings but would bring us a bit more closer to the main subject and maybe perhaps show the audience there might be something more about this man (i.e. a bag at his feet, a suitcase, a dog, etc.)</td>', 'Great capture of this gentleman taking a seat and contemplating his surroundings. The lighting and B&W tones are wonderful. I think that this contemplative portrait of this man would be even more stronger if the main subject was fully in the frame from head to toe in an vertical frame. The horizontal crop has a little too much negative space. A vertical composition could still have some the street surroundings but would bring us a bit more closer to the main subject and maybe perhaps show the audience there might be something more about this man (i.e. a bag at his feet, a suitcase, a dog, etc.)', '2015-11-02 08:46:16.000000', NULL, 1),
(7413649, 1168431, 43463, '<td valign="top">One challenging thing about street photography is sometimes showing a relationship between two seemingly separate things or parallels. This appears as just an everyday common scene that would typically not hold a viewer''s attention for long, but you do have two things within this photo that could capture your audience''s eye. They just need to be framed to better to show the relationship between them. First off moving closer and to your right would have allowed you to zoom up on the woman refueling her car. Then the picture would not have the trash can in the frame and that beam to the left. That would then allow you to have the woman refueling her car fill 50% of the composition. The other 50% would show the graveyard beyond. Cars can symbolize freedom to travel life''s roads. She is living life by refueling and will continue on to do whatever things to do that day, but for others the road of life has come to an end( as seen in that graveyard). The message could be enjoy each moment of life as it comes for the road will eventually end. A closer crop/framing of those main elements (the woman refueling and the gravesite across the street) you could title it several different ways to get your audience to see the relationship you want them to see: Rest Stop, A Pause in the Road of Life, The Road Ahead, Journeys, etc.</td>', 'One challenging thing about street photography is sometimes showing a relationship between two seemingly separate things or parallels. This appears as just an everyday common scene that would typically not hold a viewer''s attention for long, but you do have two things within this photo that could capture your audience''s eye. They just need to be framed to better to show the relationship between them. First off moving closer and to your right would have allowed you to zoom up on the woman refueling her car. Then the picture would not have the trash can in the frame and that beam to the left. That would then allow you to have the woman refueling her car fill 50% of the composition. The other 50% would show the graveyard beyond. Cars can symbolize freedom to travel life''s roads. She is living life by refueling and will continue on to do whatever things to do that day, but for others the road of life has come to an end( as seen in that graveyard). The message could be enjoy each moment of life as it comes for the road will eventually end. A closer crop/framing of those main elements (the woman refueling and the gravesite across the street) you could title it several different ways to get your audience to see the relationship you want them to see: Rest Stop, A Pause in the Road of Life, The Road Ahead, Journeys, etc.', '2015-11-02 09:14:14.000000', NULL, 1),
